From e694e6527cf9f5d06285b6f86b1796fa25c1bc77 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 01:35:01 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 25/33] super-amd: keep the state of volumes in mdmon

RAID1, RAID10 and RAID5 volumes (they have an AMD write-intent bitmap)
and RAIDABLE ones (it has none) now run read-write: mdmon keeps their
state the way the AMD driver does.

- dirty/clean in the write-intent bitmap on element [0][0]: the whole
  volume is marked while md has it active, so that the AMD driver checks
  it after a crash under Linux; after a crash under either, md resyncs
  from the first bit set;
- a member md fails is recorded as failed (element flags 0x10), a member
  missing when the volume goes into use, or when an mdmon takes the
  running volume over, as offline (0x30), and the volume CRITICAL, in a
  new configuration: the packet goes to the next slot of the ring of
  every up-to-date disk, then the commit blocks with the
  generation bumped; disks that are missing or failing are left alone
  and stay stale, and so does a member out of date (its generation
  keeps it out of the volume), while a disk that is here and holds no
  part of a volume in use - free, a spare, a member failed out - gets
  every configuration, and the live one when it is behind, as the AMD
  driver writes its configuration to every disk it finds
  (RC_WriteAllConfigData);
- the initialization (CREATE_REDUNDANCY) of a volume made in the BIOS:
  md resyncs it, its progress goes to the AMD transform log - a reboot,
  into Linux or the AMD driver, goes on from there - and its end into
  the configuration;
- a disk that joins the running container (udev adds them one at a
  time) or leaves it is taken in or dropped by the monitor.

A volume with another transform under way (a rebuild, a migration)
stays read-only.  mdmon's two threads share the metadata: one of them
at a time works on it.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5 and Claude Fable 5.2, AI models by Anthropic.
